{"body": "The most defensible defect I can locate in J is a mislocation of P9’s compatibility objection. J first says:\n\n> “P9 supplies a conditional objection whose target is not fixed by its wording.”\n\nand then classifies the objection by saying:\n\n> “That objection does not attack L. It attacks A’s independence assumption, C’s omission, and/or R’s overclaim.”\n\nBut P9’s wording does fix the object of the objection: intervals. P9 says:\n\n> “That gives an interval, but intervals lose correlations: booking A’s high alternative might be incompatible with booking B’s high alternative if they share a physical resource not named in the snapshot.”\n\nThe objection is not that the min/max interval was computed by assuming independence. The min/max interval can be computed as a bound without any independence assumption, and it remains a valid bound even when cross-booking correlations exist. The objection is that the interval may be treated as the set of feasible global availabilities when it is not. That is a representational loss, not an independence assumption in the aggregation layer. J later partly repairs this by saying:\n\n> “If the account claims that its min/max interval is the feasible range of global availability, the objection is a criticism of the aggregation rule: it assumes independent combinability.”\n\nThat conditional statement is much closer to P9. But the earlier sentence—“It attacks A’s independence assumption”—collapses the distinction between two different accounts:\n\n1. An account outputs the min/max interval and claims only that the true availability lies within it.  \n2. An account outputs the same interval and claims that every value in it is achievable.\n\nP9’s compatibility objection is decisive against (2), but not against (1). A concrete case makes this clear. Suppose capacity is 10. Booking A has local alternatives {2, 5}; booking B has local alternatives {3, 4}. A shared resource makes the combination (5, 4) impossible, but the other combinations remain possible. The per-booking min/max computation gives A ∈ {2, 5}, B ∈ {3, 4}, sum in [5, 9], so availability in [1, 5]. The jointly realizable sums are 5, 6, and 8, giving availabilities 5, 4, and 2. The interval [1, 5] contains 1 and 3, which are not achievable. But computing [1, 5] did not require assuming independence; it only failed to exclude the impossible combination. An account that asserts “availability lies in [1, 5]” is not refuted by P9. An account that asserts “availability can be any value in [1, 5]” is refuted.\n\nSo J’s initial target classification is an added relationship not supported by P9’s wording. The distinction between the object of the objection (intervals) and the P7 target category of the criticism (assumption, rule, snapshot, etc.) is relevant here. P9’s wording fixes the object; what is unfixed is which P7 category the criticism belongs to. J’s target-indexed account needs that distinction, but its first formulation blurs it. The defect is localized: J’s later conditional reading is substantially better, so this is not a global refutation of the target-indexed account. It is an established mischaracterization of the initial target, not an established failure of the entire proposal.\n\nThe selected carrier is unconstrained prose, and the frozen question explicitly asks to distinguish what the supplied material says from what the proposal adds. J does that in several places, but not at the point where it says the objection attacks an independence assumption. That addition is not marked as an addition, and it is not entailed by P9. I am not proposing a repair or editing J; I am recording the defect and leaving the broader question—whether P9’s objection requires a change to an account of global availability—conditional on the account’s claim strength.", "commitments": "The most defensible defect I can locate in J is a mislocation of P9’s compatibility objection. J first says:\n\n> “P9 supplies a conditional objection who